  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` and it passes `typecheck`. I haven't run `vite build` or rendered it in a browser, so the visuals, animation and orbit are unchecked.\n\n- **Island:** a flat-shaded, jittered heightfield with a color for each face (wet sand, sand, two greens, rock, snow). It has about 26 trees (a trunk cylinder plus two cones) and 14 rocks. The rocks are squashed 5-sided cones, so they look pointy rather than round.\n- **Water:** a custom GLSL shader on a 260 m plane with animated vertex waves. It has a Fresnel mix, a sun or moon glint, crest sparkle and a fade to the horizon color in the distance. The shader's uniforms are updated every frame.\n- **Day/night:** one full cycle takes 20 seconds, starting in daylight. The sun and moon positions, their light colors and intensities, the hemisphere light, the water colors, the fog color and a gradient sky (with stars at night) all change with the cycle. The hemisphere light stays at a minimum of 0.38 intensity, so the scene should never go black.\n- **Camera:** it sits 16 m above the water, 29 m from the center, looks at the island and orbits slowly. The orbit uses `app.camera.setPose`, which I didn't confirm works the way I assumed.\n- **Contract:** it draws into `#app` and sets `window.__EVAL_READY__ = true` after `await app.ready()`, which is slightly earlier than \"first frame drawn\".\n\nI used the gradient sky rather than the Preetham sky from the docs recipe, so I could control the colors directly. The recipe I copied the per-frame `setSky` call from uses Preetham, so the gradient path is the less proven one.\n\n",
